  "cause": "**The mandate was granted on Sunday morning, and what was handed over was a calendar.** **President Isaac Herzog** announced on **8 November** that he would task **Yashar** leader and former military chief **Gadi Eisenkot** with forming the next government, having informed both Eisenkot and **Benjamin Netanyahu** of the decision. The arithmetic behind it is the count published on Thursday: **67** of the incoming Knesset's members recommended Eisenkot in last week's consultations, **46** recommended Netanyahu. Eisenkot now has **28 days**, with a possible **14-day extension** — a deadline in early December, or just before it. **The blocs have not moved since the count.** Netanyahu's **Likud** with its far-right and ultra-Orthodox allies holds **53 seats** of 120; Eisenkot's Yashar with three other Jewish opposition parties holds **48**; the two Arab-led alliances, the **United Arab List** and the **Joint List**, won an unprecedented **19** seats on a surge in Arab turnout — and all but one of the Jewish parties pledged before the election not to sit with them. That is the entire difficulty in one sentence: **the 67 who recommended Eisenkot are not 61 who will sit in the same room.** Eisenkot wrote on X: *\"Now is the time to form a national emergency government. I will do everything to ensure we are worthy.\"* **Herzog's own words set the price of the next four weeks.** During consultations he said he expected *\"factions and their representatives to understand that, in the current reality, not every commitment and promise can be fulfilled\"* — an instruction to break campaign pledges, addressed to parties that built their campaigns on them. The precedent available is **2020**, when Netanyahu and **Benny Gantz** agreed a rotating premiership after three deadlocked elections; between **2019 and 2022** Israel held five. The **Knesset is sworn in on 10 November**, one day after the mandate lands, with the opposition already moving to contest Speaker **Amir Ohana**'s chair.",
