  "cause": "**The second large strike package in three days landed the night before the delegations fly, and the target list has not changed.** Overnight into **8 November**, Russia attacked with **more than 30 ballistic missiles and over 170 drones** across **12 regions**, with **Kyiv hit hardest**, according to President **Volodymyr Zelensky**. **Nine people were hurt nationwide, including one child.** The **North Bridge**, residential buildings and a kindergarten were damaged in the capital; parts of Kyiv lost electricity, water and heating. Mayor **Vitali Klitschko** later said the water supply was back to normal and around **60%** of the city's apartment buildings had been reconnected to heating; **Denys Shmyhal** said power cuts continued in five regions and did not say how many homes were affected. **The precise target was the switchyard, not the reactor.** Zelensky said substations *\"that connect the Rivne and Khmelnytsky nuclear power plants to Kyiv and central Ukraine\"* were struck, with further substation damage in **Vinnytsia, Zhytomyr, Rivne, Kharkiv and Dnipro** regions. That is a technical distinction with a political edge: striking the connection does not breach a containment and does not release radiation, but it can force a plant offline, and a plant forced offline in winter is a nuclear-safety event that arrives without a mushroom cloud. **Temperatures are falling and parts of the country have had their first snow.** **The talks are named, dated and two days out.** Ukrainian and Russian delegations meet in **Abu Dhabi on 10–11 November**, with the Ukrainian side having spent the weekend asking for *\"unity between both parties on issues of security, support for Ukraine, condemnation of Russian aggression, and increased sanctions pressure\"* — an appeal to bipartisanship in a Congress whose majority has just changed hands, made by a president whose cities are being rebuilt while the next package is loaded.",
